WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.168
Jacobian regularisation is one of those methods
that just works.

00:00:04.248 --> 00:00:09.079
Add a penalty on the network's input-output
Jacobian and you get a simple,

00:00:09.159 --> 00:00:11.999
strong defence against adversarial examples.

00:00:12.079 --> 00:00:15.450
The problem is that nobody could really explain why.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:20.560
The theory that had cracked open ordinary training,
the infinite-width limit,

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.002
had never been pushed to cover the Jacobian, let
alone training that penalises it.

00:00:26.082 --> 00:00:32.440
This paper asks whether that same lens can finally
explain what Jacobian regularisation is doing.
